hi this is Scott Butterworth standing in  for reviewer Peter Brown if you've ever  stepped into samus aran's boots for a  Metroid adventure you'll instantly  recognize parallels and head Landers  world structure outfits and animations  on the other hand years of experience  with Samus can't prepare you for head  Lenders lead character ahead in a  rocket-powered jar without a body to  call home  you have to decapitate robots and steal  their bodies to fight back this is but  one of many refreshing concepts on  display not least of which is a stylized  70s sci-fi aesthetic that's immediately  appealing  this side-scrolling adventure takes  place in a world populated by hedonist  robots who at any given moment are  trippin out trying to get laid or  dancing just another day in the Pleasure  Dome  you're guided through this odd world by  a man named Earl a voice on the other  side of your communication equipment  who's trying to help you escape the  clutches of Methuselah the lead AI Earl  southern drawl and lackadaisical  personality are a breath of fresh air  portraying level-headed and helpful  gentlemen in a sea of far-out characters  when Earl first jars you from your deep  sleep you awake in a state of shock  without a body's you can't speak or fire  back at gun-toting robots as you venture  through the Pleasure Dome and into the  belly of Methuselah fortress you take  over robot bodies to gain access to  weapons and security doors  robot bodies are color-coded to security  levels and lasers can be used to open  doors if you can't walk up to them with  a body intact this becomes necessary for  one very important reason you can't jump  it's a weird omission at first blush but  once you get used to flying your head  around in low gravity a satisfying  experience on its own you quickly forget  why you cared about the lack of jumping  in the first place your head has  unlimited propulsion and vacuum power  and it's graceful in flight carrying  momentum and turning on a dime if it  needs to  every victim you possess has its own  life bar but the one associated with  your helmet is the one that really  matters thankfully it replenishes  automatically over a short period of  time even if you manage to die het  lander is extremely forgiving allowing  you in most cases to respawn in the same  room the only exceptions are multistage  boss fights and objectives where you're  forced to restart from the beginning of  your task upon death this is really only  troublesome during the games - boss  fights where death comes to the drop of  a hat should you fly erratically or  wander into a projectile on your way to  methuselah doorstep you're sent on a lot  of fetch quests but it isn't as bad as  it sounds  pets contain battle sequences and  puzzles that require you to definitely  swap bodies and survive stretches of  hazardous corridors to arrive at your  destination with a specific type of  robot well the shooting could be better  it's twitchy nature isn't damming  frustration is inevitable when you're  carefully aiming shots and enemies keep  responding and firing at you but these  occasions rarely require more than two  or three attempts to overcome  once in a pop when you aren't dead set  on accomplishing a mission you can veer  the corners of maps and hunt for  upgrades or for large doses of energy  which you can invest in the abilities  that manifest when you inhabit a body  the biggest upgrade your head gets is a  powerful - but there are numerous  powerful upgrades that activate when you  pilot bodies including a rainbow-colored  armored aura and the ability to abandon  bodies that continuously fire their  weapons essentially turning them into  turrets it takes the entire game to gain  enough energy to unlock every upgrade  but it seems like incremental upgrades  for your helmet lie in wait around every  other corner the ten or so hours it  takes to beat headliner are packed with  glimmering visual effects and great  tunes that range from light-hearted  lounge music to overbearing synth tracks  it's a weird and infectious world that  feels like the perfect playground for  head Landers quirky and engaging action  there's rarely a bump in the road that  proves significant enough to disrupt the  joys of flying around as a head  decapitating robots and taking over  bodies against the game's absurd and  colorful backdrop  while Headley unders parallels to  Metroid bubble under the surface if the  game's fresh qualities that grab your  attention and make it one of the most  delightful takes on the well-worn  formula in Gears
 

